# ============================================================
#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): castle_halls.
#  Repeatable routine events for map visits to the castle's
#  public halls: courtyard, library, refectory, lecture tower,
#  great hall, infirmary, alchemy laboratory and job board.
#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.
# ============================================================

# ---------------- The Courtyard ----------------

@event courtyard_sundial_bench
time: afternoon
title: The Slow Sundial
place: courtyard
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: sun
tags: study
scene: A stone bench beside a weathered sundial in a flagstone courtyard, a student reading with their back to the dial.
text: {There's a bench by the old sundial, which runs eleven minutes slow.|Someone has scratched a tiny "+11" by the dial's noon. A fellow sufferer.|The sundial bench is yours by custom. A first-year asked if you came with it.} {season == "winter": Frost fills the hour lines, and the shadow skates across them.|{Pigeons are holding a meeting on the gnomon.|A first-year checks a watch against it, horrified.|Someone has chalked a face where noon should be.}}
> left: Read until the shadow moves
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  say: {season == "winter": {Your fingers go numb by page two. The chapter is worth it.|You read in gloves, turning pages with your teeth.|You read in your own bench's frost-free corner. A first-year brings you a hot chestnut, as a tribute to the bench.}|{You finish a chapter in the minutes the sundial owes you, squinting into the glare.|A pigeon reads over your shoulder, unimpressed, and your neck has a crick in it by the end.|The shadow crawls; you outpace it by three pages and a stiff back.}}
> right: Close your eyes a while
  mood +small
  renown -tiny
  say: {You wake with a chalk moustache and no suspects.|You wake to a prefect clearing his throat an inch from your ear.|You wake rested and late, eleven minutes late by the dial.}

@event courtyard_saved_seat
time: afternoon
title: A Seat on the Rim
place: courtyard
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: group
tags: social
cast:
  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0
scene: Two students sitting on the dry edge of a courtyard fountain, bags between them, pigeons circling overhead.
text: {{here} has claimed the dry stretch of the fountain rim and waves you over.|{here} is on your favourite stretch of the fountain rim, the one the sun reaches first, and waves you over.|Your stretch of the fountain rim is known now. {here} is sitting on it and shifts over without being asked.} {Two towers' bells are arguing about the hour.|Pigeons circle like creditors.|{season == "winter": Your breath hangs between you.|Someone's lunch smells better than yours.}}
> left: Sit down and catch up
  social here
  if npc_is(corvin):
    say: {Corvin tells you three stories, one of them true, and calls you "saint" when you don't ask which.|Corvin spots a prefect, palms something off the rim, and goes on talking as if nothing happened.|Corvin shifts over and hands you half of something he shouldn't have. "Your seat, saint. I kept it warm. Illegally."}
  elif npc_is(nessa):
    say: {Nessa interviews you about the weather, the soup and your love life, in that order, and writes down all three.|Nessa reads you tomorrow's headline and asks if it's too cruel. It is. She keeps it.|Nessa shifts over without looking up from her notes. "You're in tomorrow's column," she says. "Nicely, for once."}
  elif npc_is(bram):
    say: {Bram lists every rule broken in the courtyard while you sit. Then, gruffly, he asks how your exams went.|Bram writes a demerit for a passing first-year, then tears it up when he thinks you aren't looking.|Bram shifts over with great dignity and informs you the seat is technically unassigned. Then he guards it while you fetch a cider.}
  else:
    say: {You trade the castle's news for an hour. {here} does most of the trading.|You talk until the shadows cross the fountain. Neither of you can remember about what.|You sit on your stretch of the rim and catch up. {here} already knows half your news and makes you tell it anyway.}
  say: The cider from the cart by the gate is on you.
> right: Wave, and keep walking
  lore +small
  rel here -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You promise next time and take your book to the library instead. {here} looks as if {they} {are} counting.|You spend the hour with a book instead. It is a good book; it is not much company.|You wave and keep walking. {here} stays on your stretch of the rim, which feels like a small theft.}

@event courtyard_seasonal_game
title: The Courtyard Game
place: courtyard
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: group
tags: social
scene: Students playing a rowdy game across a flagstone courtyard while others crowd the edges shouting bets.
text: {season == "winter": A snowball war between Emberhall and Stillwater has reached the treaty stage.|{season == "spring": A charmed ball is loose, dodging everyone who kicks at it.|{season == "summer": It's too hot for sport, so it's catch with a charmed apple that bites.|It's conker season. The champion conker is said to be older than the fountain.}}} {Someone shouts for one more player.|They shout for you by name.|Both sides shout for you, and start bidding.}
> left: Join in
  challenge feat apprentice wild:
    win:
      renown +small
      mood +small
      health -tiny
      say: {Your side wins, and the result is disputed until supper.|You score the deciding point and are carried three whole steps before they drop you.|You join the side that bid more. They win, and the other side says you were bought, which you were.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood +tiny
      say: {You are flattened, politely, by both sides at once.|You spend the rest of the game as the thing the other side aims at.|Both sides think you're on theirs. Both sides find out, at once, in the middle of the courtyard.}
> right: Hold the coats and the bets
  chance even:
    gold +small
    say: {The favourite loses. You are the only person in the courtyard who is pleased about it.|The underdog wins by a whisker, and the coats you are holding are suddenly lighter on your conscience.|You hold the coats and both sides' bids. The underdog wins. You are, briefly, the richest person in the courtyard.}
  else:
    if gold > 0:
      gold -small
      say: {The favourite wins, and so does everyone who backed them, out of your pocket.|Everyone bet the same way, and everyone was right. You pay out in a hail of small coins.|The favourite wins, and both sides had bet on it through you. You pay out twice, which shouldn't be possible.}
    else:
      mood -small
      say: The favourite wins. With nothing in your pocket to pay out, you're chased twice round the fountain and lose a coat button to the crowd.

# ---------------- The Library ----------------

@event library_wandering_book
title: The Book That Moved
place: library
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: book
tags: study
scene: Tall library shelves leaning very slightly, a gap where one book should be, a student on a ladder peering into it.
text: {Yesterday your book was on the third floor. Today the third floor has never heard of it.|Your book has moved again, leaving a bookmark in the gap, like a note.|You keep a map of where the shelves hide your books. They've started hiding the map.} {In its place is a shelf of cookery spells.|In its place are bound weather reports.|In its place is one slim volume, face out, waiting.} {final_year: Soon they'll play these games with someone else.|}
> left: Hunt it down
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You find it on the sixth floor, filed under "regret".|You find it in the stairwell, halfway between floors, as if it had been trying to leave.|You find it at once. Either you are learning the shelves, or they are getting lazy.}
> right: Read what's in front of you
  mood +small
  say: {You read the wrong book with great conviction, and quote it at supper.|You learn a great deal about something nobody will ever ask you about.|The wrong book turns out to be a very good novel. You read all of it, and none of it will be on any exam.}

@event library_shared_table
title: The Last Free Chair
place: library
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: social study
cast:
  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0
scene: A crowded library reading room, every table full but one, a single empty chair facing a student absorbed in a book.
text: {The library is full. The only free chair is opposite {here}.|The only free chair is opposite {here}. A shelf has leaned over it, as if keeping it for you.|The library knows your habits now. The free chair, opposite {here}, has a book you wanted waiting on it.|The chair opposite {here} is so reliably free that you suspect the library of arranging it.} {Rain ticks on the tall windows.|A shelf nearby is quietly rearranging itself.|Footnote is asleep in the returns basket.|{season == "winter": The radiators clank like a ghost with a grudge.|Dust turns gold in the light.}}
> left: Take the chair and work
  rel here +small
  lore +small
  if npc_is(kael):
    say: {Kael doesn't look up. By the end of the hour there is frost on your inkwell and a tiny, careful correction in your margin.|Kael pushes a page of his notes across to you, then looks away as if it had moved on its own.|Kael moves his inkwell to make room for yours. By the end, there's frost on both, and a note in his hand: "Your third line."|Kael says one sentence in the whole hour. It's the answer to the question you were stuck on. He goes back to reading.}
  elif npc_is(lyra):
    say: {Lyra slides the candle an inch toward your side without looking up. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra finishes before you, and waits, and says she wasn't waiting.|Lyra corrects your notation without asking and is right. You correct hers and are also right. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra works at your pace for once, and you finish together. She says it was coincidence, twice.}
  elif npc_is(seraphine):
    say: {Seraphine writes a word in your notes that you won't understand until next week.|Seraphine reads the same page for an hour, smiling at something that isn't on it.|Seraphine slides you a book you hadn't asked for. You'll need it on Thursday, it turns out.|Seraphine works in silence, then says your name once, softly, as if checking it was still there.}
  elif npc_is(bram):
    say: {Bram informs you that you breathe too loudly. Then he lends you his ruler.|Bram underlines every rule in his book twice. Once, he asks your opinion of one.|Bram shares his lamp and lectures you on its proper use. The lecture is long. The lamp is very good.|Bram reads your essay upside down from across the table and finds the mistake. He's insufferable about it, and right.}
  else:
    say: {You work in silence for an hour, which turns out to be a kind of company.|Neither of you speaks. Both of you get further than you would have alone.|You work opposite {here} as if you'd planned to. By the end you've both got further, and nodded once.|You share the table for the hour and the cat for the last ten minutes. It's a good arrangement.}
> right: Whisper a question
  rel here +small
  mood +small
  renown -tiny
  say: {The conversation is worth it. The look from the front desk has a long memory.|You get three sentences in before a shush arrives from two floors up.|You whisper your question to {here} and get a whispered answer. The front desk lets it pass, this once.|Your whisper wakes the cat in the returns basket, who complains loudly. You are shushed for the cat's noise.}

@event library_overdue
title: Overdue
place: library
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: book
tags: study money
scene: A student holding a book behind their back near a wicker returns basket, a cat on the front desk watching them.
text: {The book you borrowed in a hurry is overdue, and the library knows it. The spine has started to sigh.|Another overdue book. The front desk has begun keeping a little card with your name on it.|Your overdue books sigh in chorus now, and the card at the front desk has a second side.} {Footnote watches you approach the returns basket with professional interest.|The front desk is, for once, unattended.|A queue of first-years is paying fines in small, sad coins.}
> left: Own up and pay the fine
  alignment +tiny
  lore +small
  if gold > 0:
    gold -small
    say: {The fine is paid; the desk, mollified, lends you the sequel.|You pay, and are lent another book, which you suspect is a trap.|The desk sighs, takes your coins and hands you a book it says you'll like. It is always right, which is the annoying part.}
  else:
    mood -small
    say: You own up with empty pockets. The desk lets you work the fine off reshelving, which takes all evening, and lends you the sequel anyway.
> right: Slip it into the basket
  alignment -tiny
  challenge deceive apprentice:
    win:
      guile +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {The book goes in without a sound. It stops sighing at once, which feels like forgiveness.|You drop it in under cover of a sneeze. Footnote saw. Footnote will keep it to himself, for a price.|You slip it in while the first-years pay their fines. Nobody notices one more sad sound.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -tiny
      if gold > 0:
        gold -medium
        say: {The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. You pay double.|It lands with a groan. The front desk adds a mark to your card, and the fine, doubled.|The book sighs in harmony with your other overdue books. The desk adds a third side to your card.}
      else:
        say: The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. With nothing to pay, you're put on reshelving until the fine is worked off twice over.

# ---------------- The Refectory ----------------

@event refectory_late_supper
slot: evening
title: Late Supper
place: refectory
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: meal
tags: home
scene: The far end of a long refectory table at night, most benches empty, a single student with a bowl and a half-written letter.
text: {You come in late. The benches are empty and the cooks are wiping the far tables.|The cook leaves a lamp lit at the end of the bench now, where the draught can't find you.|Your late bowl is waiting under a plate, with a spoon laid beside it. Nobody admits to it.} {year == 1: Home feels very far away tonight.|{final_year: You count the suppers you have left, and stop.|{The candles are down to stubs.|Someone has left a half-done puzzle on the next table.|The cook is humming in the kitchen behind the hatch.}}}
> left: Write a letter home
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {You write two pages and post them with the last of your coppers.|You write about the food. You do not write about anything else, but they'll know.|You write a letter that is mostly questions. You post it before you can make it braver.}
> right: Revise over the bowl
  lore +small
  say: {You learn three dates and a spill of gravy across all of them.|The cook reads over your shoulder and corrects one of your answers. She is right.|You revise over your bowl. The cook takes the plate away when you've finished and leaves a second helping.}

@event refectory_house_tables
time: evening
title: Where to Sit
place: refectory
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: group
tags: social
scene: A crowded refectory at suppertime, a student standing with a tray between a loud house table and a quieter one of strangers.
text: {Supper, and the question of where to sit.|Your house table has started saving a gap on the bench, roughly your width.|The gap is a named seat now, and the quiet table nods at you too.} Your house table is {loud with a story about a goat|in the middle of a pudding treaty|singing, badly}. At the far end, {season == "winter": some second-years from Rootward huddle round a warming charm.|some second-years from Duskgate are arguing quietly about a map.}
> left: Squeeze in with your house
  mood +small
  renown +tiny
  say: {You eat with an elbow in your ribs and laugh the whole way through.|Somebody's story gets better every time you hear it. So does the pudding.|You squeeze into your gap and join the singing, badly, to fit in. It works.}
> right: Sit with the strangers
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {They are polite, then curious, then actually rather good company.|It is awkward for exactly one course. By pudding, they have told you their secret map route.|The quiet table makes room as if you'd always sat there. They ask about your house's singing, with sympathy.}

@event refectory_pudding_tax
time: evening
title: The Pudding Tax
place: refectory
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cup
tags: social
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect)) and around(friend)
scene: Two friends at a refectory table, one guarding a bowl of treacle pudding, the other holding a spoon at the ready.
text: {{friend} sits down opposite you and looks at your pudding the way a cat looks at a fish.|Your pudding has a reputation now. {friend} sits down opposite you with a spoon already in hand.|Word of your pudding tax has got round the refectory. Tonight {friend} comes to collect, with a little receipt.} {season == "winter": Tonight it's treacle sponge, steaming.|{Tonight it's apple crumble.|Tonight it's a custard that wobbles with intent.|Tonight it's jam roly-poly, much defended.}}
> left: Pay the pudding tax
  rel friend +small
  mood -small
  if npc_is(tobias):
    say: {Tobias apologises four times, eats it all, then tells you a long story about a goat who did the same. It helps, somehow.|Tobias eats half, then pushes the bowl back with a sprig of mint from his pocket on top, as an apology.|Tobias pays you back with a receipt of his own: half a jar of honey from home. You call it even.}
  elif npc_is(lyra):
    say: {"Efficient," Lyra says, finishing it. The next day a whole pudding appears on your tray. She doesn't mention it.|Lyra eats it in four exact bites and critiques the custard. She does not give it back.|Lyra presents the receipt, takes exactly the tax and not a crumb more. She is, you have to admit, scrupulous.}
  elif npc_is(mir):
    say: {Mir eats it while explaining a pudding-defence device that would probably work. Probably.|Mir eats it with a spoon they built themselves, which hums. They say the humming is probably normal.|Mir hands you a receipt they've engraved on brass, collects, and promises a refund mechanism by spring.}
  else:
    say: {{friend} eats exactly half and leaves you the burnt corner.|{friend} eats it slowly, savouring your defeat.|{friend} collects the tax and writes "paid" on the receipt. You're starting to suspect the receipts are the point.}
> right: Defend your pudding
  challenge deceive vs friend.guile:
    win:
      mood +small
      rel friend -tiny
      say: {You hide it under a napkin and point at the window. It works. {friend} will remember this.|You claim it's been sneezed on. {friend} retreats in disgust, and you eat in peace.|You refuse to recognise the receipt. {friend} appeals to the table, which rules in your favour, narrowly.}
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      rel friend +tiny
      say: {{friend} sees straight through you and takes the whole thing as a penalty. It is hard to stay cross.|{friend} distracts you with a story and the bowl is empty before it ends.|You dispute the receipt. {friend} produces a second receipt for the dispute, and takes the pudding as costs.}

# ---------------- The Lecture Tower ----------------

@event lecture_tower_old_board
title: Someone Else's Lecture
place: lecture_tower
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: scroll
tags: study
scene: An empty spiral lecture hall, a blackboard still dense with chalk diagrams from a class that has long gone.
text: {term: {The fourth-floor hall is empty, but someone else's lecture is still on the board.|In the empty hall, a stub of chalk waits on the ledge, as if for you.|The board has a corner fenced off in chalk, labelled "answers here".}|In the break the boards are wiped, except one nobody dares touch.} {Today it's a ward diagram, labelled "wrong, but how?"|Today it's half a proof, ending in a thumbprint.|Today it's a list headed "on the exam", crossed out.}
> left: Copy it all down
  arcana +small
  say: {Your hand cramps. Your head, for once, does not.|You copy it all and understand most of it. The rest you'll grow into.|You copy the board, fenced corner and all. Your answer from last time is still there, with a tick beside it.}
> right: Add your own answer
  chance likely:
    renown +small
    say: {Next time, someone has added "yes!" under your line.|Someone has answered your answer. The board has become a correspondence.|By the afternoon someone has drawn a box around your line, to keep it safe.}
  else:
    renown -tiny
    mood -tiny
    say: {The professor whose board it was finds you holding the chalk. "Hm," says the look.|Your answer is wiped by the time you reach the door, by someone who knew better.|You write in the "answers here" corner. The next morning there's a line through it, and "nearly", underlined.}

@event lecture_tower_stair_question
title: One Question a Flight
place: lecture_tower
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: tower
tags: study
cast:
  prof: thorne
scene: A narrow spiral stair inside a stone tower, a severe professor climbing ahead with a stack of papers, a student a step behind.
text: {You meet Professor Thorne on the spiral stair. He allows one question per flight, and climbs fast.|Professor Thorne on the stair. He has slowed down by about one step in seven, which is how you know he expects you.|Thorne hands you half his stack of papers at the foot of the stair, without a word, and starts climbing.} {term: |The tower is half empty for the break; your footsteps echo twice.}{final_year: There are not many of these climbs left.|}
> left: Ask a hard question
  challenge research using arcana adept-:
    win:
      arcana +small
      rel prof +tiny
      say: {He answers in four words, and the four words are enough.|"Hm," he says, then answers it properly, all the way to the seventh floor.|You carry half his papers and ask your question on the fourth flight. He answers on the seventh, and it was worth the wait.}
    lose:
      arcana +tiny
      mood -small
      say: {He answers with a harder question and leaves you on the landing.|"Wrong question," he says. "Find the right one by Thursday."|He takes his papers back on the landing. "Better question next time," he says, which means he'll be expecting one.}
> right: Climb in silence beside him
  rel prof +small
  health -tiny
  say: {He climbs faster than you. At the top he says, "Adequate," and you are fairly sure he means the climbing.|Neither of you says a word for seven flights. It is oddly restful, apart from your legs.|You carry his papers in silence. At the top he takes them back and says, "Thursday," which is an invitation.}

@event lecture_tower_scorched_room
title: The Scorched Room
place: lecture_tower
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: flame
tags: magic danger
scene: A practice room on a lower floor of a stone tower, walls streaked with scorch marks, a sign by the door forbidding unsupervised casting.
text: {The practice room on the Evocation floor is empty. A sign forbids unsupervised casting.|The scorched room is empty. Since your last visit, someone has underlined the sign.|The scorched room. The sign is underlined three times now, and signed by the headmistress.} {A fresh scorch mark is shaped like a surprised face.|Someone has left a bucket of sand by the door.|{season == "winter": The walls are the only warm thing here.|The air smells faintly of singed eyebrow.}}
> left: Practise anyway
  mana -small
  chance likely:
    arcana +small
    say: {Your spell lands clean in the centre of the oldest scorch mark. You leave no evidence.|You practise until the flame does what you mean, not what you say.|You practise under the headmistress's signature. Your spell is very good. You leave before it's tempted to be better.}
  else:
    health -small
    renown -tiny
    say: {A new scorch mark joins the wall, shaped very like you.|The spell comes back at you. You leave with your eyebrows, mostly.|Your scorch mark lands right under the headmistress's signature. You'd swear the signature looks disappointed.}
> right: Work through the theory
  lore +small
  say: {You draw the spell on paper fifty times. Paper is very forgiving.|You learn exactly why the walls look like that, and resolve not to add to them.|You work the theory under the thrice-underlined sign. You'll cast it properly, supervised, and it will be perfect.}

# ---------------- The Great Hall ----------------

@event great_hall_tomorrow_sky
title: Tomorrow's Weather
place: great_hall
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: magic
scene: An empty great hall in the evening, floating candles drifting under a vaulted ceiling that shows a different sky from the windows.
text: {The great hall is empty but for the candles. The ceiling shows tomorrow.|One candle drifts over to you and hovers at reading height.|Your candle meets you at the door now and leads you to your bench.} {season == "winter": Tomorrow, it says, it will snow.|{season == "spring": Tomorrow, a storm with bad manners.|{season == "summer": Tomorrow is cloudless, stars and all.|Tomorrow is fog, rolling across the vaults.}}} {final_year: There are fewer tomorrows here now.|}
> left: Lie on a bench and watch
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You watch tomorrow until your neck stiffens. It is worth it.|A candle drifts down to see if you are all right, and stays.|You fall half asleep and dream tomorrow a little early.}
> right: Read by the floating candles
  lore +small
  say: {A candle drips wax onto the best paragraph. You read it anyway, through the wax.|The candles follow your page like curious moths. It is the best light in the castle.|Your candle sits on the bench beside you like a friend. The light is steady, and you read further than you meant to.}

@event great_hall_choir_rehearsal
time: evening
title: The Choir Rehearses
place: great_hall
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: music
tags: social
scene: A choir rehearsing on the stage of a candlelit great hall, a few singers floating a foot off the boards, the benches mostly empty.
text: {The choir is rehearsing on the stage, a foot off the floor, and one voice short.|Still a voice short. The conductor has left a spare songbook on your bench.|The spare songbook has your name pencilled in it, and the alto line marked in red.} {season == "winter": They are learning the Midwinter music.|{Tonight it's a round that nobody can find the end of.|Tonight it's a lament, sung far too cheerfully.|Tonight it's a song for the lake, sung very softly.}}
> left: Listen from the back
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {The collection hat comes round. It is worth every coin.|You listen until the candles gutter. The hat comes round twice.|You listen from the back with your name pencilled into a book you're not singing from. It's the best of both.}
> right: Fill the missing voice
  challenge negotiate apprentice:
    win:
      renown +small
      charm +tiny
      say: {You hit every note but one. The conductor says it was the best one.|You float a little off the floor on the last chord. Nobody mentions it, which is how you know.|You sing your marked alto line and the choir is whole. The conductor doesn't say anything, and doesn't need to.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {You come in confidently, in the wrong key, in the wrong song. The choir is very kind about it, which is worse.|You sing the round in a straight line and finish alone. The conductor thanks you for your enthusiasm.|You sing the red line, which is the soprano line, it turns out. The choir carries you, out of kindness.}

@event great_hall_high_table_dare
title: The High Table Dare
place: great_hall
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: crown
tags: social danger
scene: An empty high table on a dais at the end of a dark great hall, a carved chair at its centre, students daring each other from the doorway.
text: {Someone dares you to sit in the headmistress's chair at the empty high table.|The dare comes round again, and someone has started keeping a tally in chalk on the doorframe.|The tally on the doorframe has your column and everyone else's. First-years dare each other to dare you.} {The chair is still warm.|A cushion embroidered with an owl sits on it.|Her teacup has been left behind, still steaming, which is worse.}
> left: Take the dare
  challenge deceive apprentice wild:
    win:
      renown +small
      mood +small
      say: {You sit, cross your legs, and sip the air. The doorway erupts.|You sit for a full minute. The chair is surprisingly comfortable, and very slightly warm.|You take the dare, and lift the steaming teacup, and drink. Nobody in the doorway breathes. It's very good tea.}
    lose:
      renown -small
      say: {As you sit, a quiet, delighted laugh comes from the dark beyond the dais. You leave very fast.|A portrait above the table clears its throat. You have never moved so fast in your life.|You reach for the steaming teacup and a voice behind you says, "That's mine." You don't look round. You simply leave.}
> right: Tell them you value your life
  renown -tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: {They call you a coward, fondly. You keep your dignity, and your place at the academy.|"Coward," they chant. You bow. It seems the safest thing to do in that hall.|You decline, and point out the steaming cup. The first-years go very quiet, and so does the dare.}

# ---------------- The Infirmary ----------------

@event infirmary_spare_bed
title: The Spare Bed
place: infirmary
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: bed
tags: home
scene: A row of white infirmary beds under a high arched window, one turned down, a stern healer pointing at it.
text: {You only came in for a plaster.|Your name is on the slate by the end bed, in pencil.|Your name on the slate is in ink now.} {health < 40: Sister Amaranth takes one look at you and points at the spare bed.|Sister Amaranth decides, over her spectacles, that you'll live.} {It smells of lavender and singed wool.|Two beds down, someone hiccups in a minor key.|{season == "winter": A warming charm hums under every blanket.|The curtains breathe.}}
> left: Lie down for an hour
  mood +small
  health +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {You sleep the deep, dreamless sleep of the infirmary. The tonic afterwards costs a few crowns and tastes of regret.|You wake under a blanket you didn't pull up. There is a tonic on the side, and a bill under it.|Your name is on the slate in ink, so you take the end bed without asking. The tonic is waiting, and so is the bill.}
> right: Help roll bandages
  alchemy +small
  say: {Sister Amaranth teaches you the knot she uses. You will never tie your boots any other way.|You roll bandages and listen to her opinion of duelling, which is long and specific.|You roll bandages as if you work here. Sister Amaranth says that if you're going to be here this often you may as well.}

@event infirmary_visiting_hour
title: Visiting Hour
place: infirmary
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cross
tags: social
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect)) and around(friend)
scene: A friend propped up in an infirmary bed by a window, a visitor on a stool beside them with a paper bag of grapes.
text: {Visiting hour. {friend} is in the bed by the window,|Visiting hour. Sister Amaranth waves you to the stool you used last time. Today it's {friend},|Visiting hour. The stool by the window has worn to your shape. Today it's {friend},} {nursing a duelling bruise the colour of plums.|recovering from a draught of hiccups that hiccups back.|wrapped in three blankets against a lake chill.} {They} brighten{s} when you come in.
> left: Sit and keep {them} company
  social friend
  say: {You bring grapes and eat most of them yourself. {friend} doesn't mind.|You stay until Sister Amaranth sweeps you out. {friend} waves from the pillows.|You sit on your worn stool and tell {friend} everything they've missed. {They} fall{s} asleep halfway, smiling.}
> right: Smuggle in a pastry
  chance likely:
    rel friend +small
    mood +tiny
    say: {The pastry makes it past the desk. {friend} eats it under the blanket like a crime.|You hide the pastry in a hollowed-out book. {friend} is delighted by both.|You smuggle in a hot pastry under your coat. {friend} eats it wrapped in all three blankets and declares you a hero.}
  else:
    rel friend +tiny
    renown -tiny
    say: {Sister Amaranth confiscates the pastry and eats it herself, slowly, while looking at you.|Sister Amaranth smells the pastry from across the ward and holds out her hand without a word.|Sister Amaranth takes the pastry, cuts it in three, and gives you, {friend} and herself a piece each. Then she lectures you both.}

@event infirmary_tonic_cupboard
title: The Tonic Cupboard
place: infirmary
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: potion
tags: danger
scene: A tall glass-fronted cupboard of coloured tonics in an empty infirmary, its door ajar, a teacup steaming on the healer's desk.
text: {The tonic cupboard is open, and Sister Amaranth is in the back having her tea.|The tonic cupboard is open at teatime. A new label on the door reads "counted daily".|At teatime the cupboard is open, and the label now reads "counted daily, by name". The bright blue bottles have moved to the front.} {mana < 20: Your head aches with the dry ache of spent magic.|{The bottles clink in the draught.|A label reads, in her handwriting, "absolutely not".|The bright blue bottles have a new lock.}}
> left: Help yourself to a tonic
  alignment -tiny
  challenge deceive apprentice+:
    win:
      mana +small
      health +tiny
      guile +tiny
      say: {You drink it in the corridor. It tastes of peppermint and getting away with things.|You pocket the bottle, rinse it later, and put it back full of water. You feel slightly worse about that.|You take a blue one from the front, since that's where they've been put. You suspect that was the point.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      renown -tiny
      say: {"That one is for goats," says Sister Amaranth from the doorway. She makes you drink the right one, which is worse.|Sister Amaranth's teacup appears at your elbow. "Put it back," she says, "and sit down."|The blue bottles were put at the front to see who'd take one. Sister Amaranth writes your name in the "counted daily" book.}
> right: Ask for one properly
  health +small
  mana +tiny
  if gold > 0:
    gold -small
    say: {She charges you, lectures you, and gives you the good one.|She charges you, lectures you a little less, and gives you the good one.|"You again," she says, and hands it over before you ask.}
  else:
    mood -small
    say: She looks at your empty purse, gives you the good one anyway, and lectures you twice as long.

# ---------------- The Alchemy Laboratory ----------------

@event alchemy_lab_spare_cauldron
title: A Spare Cauldron
place: alchemy_lab
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cauldron
tags: study magic
scene: A long laboratory bench in the afternoon light, a small cauldron free among larger ones, jars of ingredients lined up beside it.
text: {There is a spare cauldron on the second bench, unclaimed.|The spare cauldron has a chalk mark on its rim like your initial.|Your cauldron, everyone calls it. Someone has scratched your initial into the handle.} {Someone has left a ladle standing upright in something.|The bench smells of mint and burnt sugar.|{season == "summer": The heat makes every brew twice as eager.|The windows have steamed over with other people's experiments.}}
> left: Brew a practice batch
  challenge brew apprentice:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {The brew turns the right colour at the right moment. It feels like being trusted.|It comes out clear and smells of nothing, which is exactly right.|You brew in your cauldron, by your initial. It knows your hand by now and doesn't argue.}
    lose:
      alchemy +tiny
      health -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {The brew turns a colour you have never seen. So do your eyebrows.|It foams over and sets, firmly, around your sleeve.|Even your own cauldron turns on you today. The brew sets, and your initial is the only part of the handle you can see.}
> right: Scrub the cauldrons for crowns
  gold +tiny
  alchemy +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {You learn more about failed potions from their residue than from any lecture.|You scrub until the bench is clean. Then you scratch your own initials into it, small, under the rim.|You scrub every cauldron and yours last, with care. It shines. Someone else uses it the next morning, and you mind.}

@event alchemy_lab_third_floor
title: The Third-Floor Fumes
place: alchemy_lab
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: bottle
tags: social magic
scene: A crowded upper floor of an alchemy laboratory, coloured vapour hanging under the ceiling, students working with handkerchiefs tied over their faces.
text: {On the third floor the ventilation charm has given up, and the fumes have taken charge.|Someone has hung a sign by the stairs giving the fumes' mood of the day.|The fumes' sign has a column for forecasts now, in several hands.} {Everyone is speaking in rhyme and trying to hide it.|Everyone is laughing at nothing and cannot stop.|Everyone is being unusually honest about everyone else's hair.} {season == "winter": The windows are frozen shut.|}
> left: Stay and work in the fug
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You get a great deal done and agree with everyone about everything.|You leave with good notes and a headache that hums.|You work through the honesty fumes and hear what everyone thinks of your hair. Your notes are excellent. Your hair is fine.}
> right: Force the windows open
  vigor +tiny
  renown +tiny
  say: {season == "winter": {The ice cracks. Half the floor cheers; half complains of the cold.|The frozen latch gives, and so does your thumbnail.|The window cracks open and the cold rushes in. Everyone stops being honest at once, with relief.}|{The windows give. The fumes leave, sulking, and the floor applauds.|You wedge the window with a textbook. The fumes drift out, and nearly the textbook too.|You open the window and the honest fumes leave. The silence that follows is extremely polite.}}

@event alchemy_lab_unlabelled_jar
title: The Unlabelled Jar
place: alchemy_lab
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: potion
tags: magic danger
scene: A dusty shelf of ingredient jars in an alchemy laboratory, one jar with no label glowing faintly, a student reaching for it.
text: {Someone has left an unlabelled jar on the shared shelf. It glows, faintly.|Another unlabelled jar on the shared shelf, beside the cautious label someone wrote for the last one.|The shared shelf now has a row of careful labels, and one new jar at the end of it, waiting its turn.} {It smells of thunder.|Something inside it turns over when you look.|It is warm to the touch.} {gpa >= 3: You could probably identify it, given time.|You have no idea what it is.}
> left: Add a pinch to your brew
  mana -tiny
  chance even:
    mana +medium
    alchemy +tiny
    say: {The brew turns gold and so do you, briefly. You feel enormous.|The brew sings a single note and leaves you humming with power.|A pinch of the warm stuff and your brew purrs like a cat. You don't know what it did, but it's better.}
  else:
    health -small
    say: {The brew ignites, politely, and then impolitely.|Your cauldron sneezes. So, for the rest of the afternoon, do you.|The warm stuff makes your brew too warm, and then your bench, and then your sleeve.}
> right: Label it, carefully
  lore +small
  say: {An hour of tests later, you write a cautious label. Someone adds a skull to it.|You work out what it is. Then you wash your hands three times.|You label it and add it to the row. Yours is the most careful label on the shelf, and the most honest: "Warm. Unsure."}

# ---------------- The Job Board ----------------

@event job_board_odd_jobs
title: Odd Jobs
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: job money
scene: A cork board in an entrance hall, a small notice pinned at an angle under the official job postings, a student reading it with a frown.
text: {Under the proper postings, someone has pinned an odd job.|Under the proper postings is an odd job, and a second pin someone has left beside it, for answers.|The odd-jobs corner has a card addressed to you by name, in a hand you're starting to recognise.} {Someone wants a homesick portrait read to.|Someone wants a ladder held, no questions asked.|Someone wants a reluctant gargoyle walked.} {term: |In the break the board is half bare, and the odd jobs pay a little better.}
> left: Take the job
  gold +small
  mood -tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {It takes all afternoon and pays exactly what it said, which is rarer than it should be.|It is stranger than the notice suggested, and pays slightly less.|You take the gargoyle for its walk. It doesn't want to go, and then it doesn't want to come back. The pay is fair.}
> right: Haggle first
  challenge negotiate apprentice:
    win:
      gold +small
      gold +tiny
      charm +tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {You haggle the fee up by a third. Your employer seems to respect you more for it.|You agree a price and a biscuit. The biscuit turns out to be the better half of the deal.|You haggle with the hand you recognise. They pay what you ask, and leave a note: "Worth it."}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {Your employer takes the notice down and pins up a new one, asking for "someone less difficult".|The job goes to a first-year who asked for nothing. They seem very happy with it.|You haggle, and the next card in that hand is addressed to someone else. You've been dropped, politely.}

@event job_board_lost_familiars
title: Lost and Found
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: cat
tags: social
scene: The corner of a cork board crowded with hand-drawn posters of lost familiars and a ransom note for a sock.
text: {The corner of the board for lost things is busier than the jobs.|An old poster in the lost-things corner now reads "found, thanks to a student".|The lost-things corner has thank-you notes now. One draws the rescuer with a halo.} {A toad called "Sir" is missing, last seen being brave.|A ransom note demands three biscuits for one sock.|A small owl is lost, answers to nothing, and bites.} {season == "winter": Half the familiars have simply gone somewhere warm.|}
> left: Go looking for it
  chance even:
    gold +small
    mood +small
    say: {You find it behind a radiator in the east tower, deeply unrepentant. The reward is small and grateful.|You find it, and the owner cries, and the reward is mostly biscuits.|You find the owl in the rafters of the owlery, pretending to belong. It bites you. The owner pays double, for the bite.}
  else:
    health -tiny
    mood -tiny
    say: {You search for an hour and find only a different, much angrier creature.|You don't find it. The next day the poster says "Found! Thanks anyway."|You find a small owl. It is not the small owl. It also bites.}
> right: Read every notice twice
  guile +small
  say: {The board is the castle's diary. You learn who lost what, and who owes whom.|You learn three secrets from the small print, and who has terrible handwriting.|You read every notice, including the thank-you notes. One of them is about you. You read that one three times.}

# Job board stories (playtest 2, N13): small, everyday, plot-safe.

@event job_board_yellow_notice
title: The Oldest Notice
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: job social
scene: A corner of a cork board where one notice, yellowed and curling, is pinned beneath years of newer ones.
text: In the bottom corner of the board, under years of newer notices, is one gone yellow and soft: "Wanted, Thursdays: someone to read the newssheet aloud to the portrait on the west stair. He likes the sport and the obituaries. Small pay." Someone has renewed the pin.
> left: Answer it, this Thursday
  gold +tiny
  mood +small
  study -tiny
  say: The portrait is a bald man in an old-fashioned collar who hasn't had the news read to him in years. He cheers the duelling results and sighs at the obituaries. You lose your Thursday evening, and he asks you to come back.
> right: Ask the porter who pins it
  lore +small
  say: The porter says the notice was there when he started, and that a few crowns appear on the stair every Thursday whether anyone reads or not. He has never asked who leaves it. He suggests you don't either.

@event job_board_torn_notice
title: Half a Notice
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: light
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: job money
scene: Two students tugging at either end of a single job notice in front of a crowded cork board, the paper starting to tear.
text: A well-paid notice ("one evening's portering, generous rates") has drawn two students, who have both got hold of it. It tears down the middle as you arrive. One holds the half with the pay; the other holds the half with the address. Both look at you.
> left: Settle it between them
  charm +small
  say: You suggest they share the evening and split the fee. They both hate the idea, then both agree to it, then both thank you. You get nothing but a reputation for being reasonable.
> right: Read the address and go yourself
  gold +small
  alignment -tiny
  renown -tiny
  say: You read the address over a shoulder and get there first. The job pays well. By supper both of them have told everyone what you did.

@event job_board_monthly_letter
title: A Letter a Month
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
weight: 10
art: letter
tags: job social
scene: A small handwritten notice pinned beside a sealed letter on a cork board, a student reaching to unpin both.
text: A small notice in a careful, shaky hand: "Wanted: a student to post a letter for me in the village, first of each month. A few crowns. I can't manage the hill any more." The letter is pinned beside it, sealed and addressed to someone in the village.
> left: Take the letter down the hill
  gold +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: You post it, then the next, then the next. In the third month the letter-writer leaves a note: the replies have started coming. You never learn who either of them is. You keep the crowns in a jar.
> right: Leave it for someone else
  say: You leave it on the board. The next morning it's gone, and someone has drawn a small heart beside the notice in pencil.

# Odd gigs (feedback 2026-10-15): the Job Board shows its job menu on every visit and one of these after it, so a
# visit always has something small to take or leave. Everyday and plot-safe; each repeats every few months.

@event job_board_copying
title: Fair Copies Wanted
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: scroll
tags: job money
scene: A cork board with a notice in a sick student's shaky hand, a stack of borrowed lecture notes tied with string beneath it.
text: {A notice in a shaky hand. A student down with fever needs a fortnight of lecture notes copied out fair, and will pay for neatness.|Another fever, another fortnight of notes to copy fair. The notice says "neat hand preferred", and someone has written your name beside it.|The copying notice again. By now you can copy a page without reading a word of it, if you want to.}
> left: Copy them for the pay
  gold +small
  mood -small
  say: {Two evenings of careful letters. The pay arrives with a note that says your sevens are beautiful.|You copy every page, blot nothing and are paid exactly. Your wrist aches until the weekend.|You copy without reading, as fast as the ink allows. The pay is fair and the evening is gone.}
> right: Copy them slowly, and learn them too
  gold +tiny
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You read as you copy. Half the pay goes on candles, and you learn a course you aren't even taking.|You copy slowly and read every line. The notes are better than your own, and you keep a few of the ideas.|You read it all, slowly. The student pays a little less for the delay, and you know a little more for it.}

@event job_board_message_run
title: Messages Across the Castle
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: letter
tags: job money
scene: A student with a satchel of folded notes running across a castle bridge between towers, a staircase swinging away behind them.
text: {A professor wants a runner for the afternoon, with notes for every tower before the bell. A small fee, and no reading them.|The runner's notice is back, with a longer list of towers.|The runner's notice, and a list you could walk in your sleep. The staircases have learned your face.}
> left: Run the round yourself
  gold +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You reach the last tower as the bell goes. Your legs are jelly and the fee is honest.|Seven towers, two moving staircases and one argument with a portrait. Paid in full.|You take the shortcuts the staircases allow you now. Paid, and back in time for supper.}
> right: Send the owls instead
  gold +tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: {You pay the owlery out of the fee and keep the rest, and the afternoon. The professor never knows.|The owls do it faster than you would have. You pocket what's left and feel only slightly guilty.|The owls know the round now as well as you. You keep a little of the fee and all of your afternoon.}

@event job_board_market_stall
title: Mind the Stall
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: coin
tags: job money
scene: A village market stall heaped with jars and woollens, a student behind it with a cash tin, the owner hobbling away towards a chair in the sun.
text: {A villager with a bad knee wants someone to mind her market stall on Saturday morning, for a share of the takings.|The stall again: jars, woollens, the bad knee and the share of the takings.|The stall owner pins the notice up with your name already on it, and a question mark, to be polite.}
> left: Mind it as she asked
  gold +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You sell three jars and a scarf and count the tin twice. She pays you a fair share and a jar.|A slow morning, honestly counted. She is pleased with the tin and gives you your share.|You know the prices by heart now. She hands you your share without counting it, which is a kind of compliment.}
> right: Talk up the wares
  gold +tiny
  charm +small
  say: {You sell like a pedlar and the takings are up. Most of the extra goes back to her, as it should, and you've learned a patter.|You talk a farmer into two scarves he didn't know he needed. Your share is small and your voice is gone.|You could sell her jars to anyone now. She jokes about leaving you the stall.}

@event job_board_lost_property
title: The Lost-Property Box
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: key
tags: job money
scene: The porter's lodge, a vast wooden box of odd gloves, scarves, books and one boot, a student sorting them into piles.
text: {The porter wants the lost-property box sorted before it starts to smell, and pays by the hour.|The lost-property box is full again, the porter says, mostly of the same gloves.|The porter slides the box across without a word. You know where the odd boot goes now.}
> left: Sort it quickly for the pay
  gold +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {Gloves, scarves, books and one boot, in piles by dusk. The porter pays and keeps the boot for himself.|You sort it in an hour. The porter pays for two, out of habit.|You sort it fast. The porter pays, and then tries the odd boot on again, hopefully.}
> right: Read what people lose
  gold +tiny
  guile +small
  say: {You learn who carries love letters and who never returns library books. The porter pays you less for taking so long.|You read the names in the books and the notes in the pockets, and put everything back exactly. A slow hour, and a useful one.|You know the castle by what it drops. The porter pays a little less, and you put the gossip away for later.}

@event job_board_sick_familiar
title: A Sick Familiar
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: cat
tags: job money
scene: A student sitting up by lamplight beside a basket where a small sick familiar is curled in a blanket, a bottle of tonic on the table.
text: {A student going home for a funeral needs someone to sit up with their sick familiar for two nights. They will pay.|Another notice for a night sitter, for a sick toad this time, and the pay is better.|The familiars' night-sitter: that's you, apparently. A cat this time, and a worried owner.}
> left: Sit up with it, both nights
  gold +small
  health -small
  say: {Two nights of tonic every hour and very little sleep. It recovers. So, eventually, do you.|The toad recovers and you nearly don't. The owner pays double and calls you a saint.|The cat is ungrateful and lives. You're paid, and spend the next day asleep in lectures.}
> right: Sit up one night, and find a second sitter
  gold +tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: {You take the first night and talk a friend into the second. The pay splits; the sleep doesn't.|One night each, you and a sleepy first-year. The familiar is fine. Half the pay is still pay.|You know who will sit up for a little money now. You split the nights and the pay, and sleep.}

@event job_board_porters_crates
title: Crates for the Porter
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: house
tags: job money
scene: A cart of wooden crates at the foot of a long castle stair, a student lifting one while the porter points upward.
text: {The porter needs crates carried up from the barge, a great many of them, up a great many stairs.|Crates again, the porter says, pointing at the barge. More of them than last time.|You hear the barge bell and the porter waves before you're even down the stairs. Crates.}
> left: Carry them all
  gold +small
  health -small
  say: {Forty crates up seventy stairs. The porter pays you and a little extra for not dropping the one that clinked.|Your back will remember every crate. The pay is good.|You carry the lot and don't count them any more. Paid, and sore.}
> right: Carry what you can, and train with it
  gold +tiny
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You take them slowly, two steps at a time, and count it as exercise. The porter pays for the crates you managed.|Half the crates, carried properly. Your legs are stronger and your purse a little lighter than it could be.|You carry what you can and stop before your back does. Paid for half, and stronger for it.}

@event job_board_tutor_hour
title: An Hour of Tutoring
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: book
tags: job money
scene: An empty classroom, a nervous first-year with an essay full of crossings-out and an older student pointing at a line.
text: {A first-year's notice, written with great care, asks for an hour's help with an essay. They can pay a little.|The same first-year's careful handwriting: another essay, another hour.|The first-year has stopped writing notices. They just pin up the essay, with a coin taped to the corner.}
> left: Take the hour, and the coin
  gold +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {An hour of commas and confusion. The essay improves; so does your purse, slightly.|You take them through it line by line. They pay, and thank you twice.|You know their mistakes before they make them now. The hour goes quickly, and they pay.}
> right: Help, and leave the coin pinned
  mood +small
  alignment +tiny
  say: {You leave the coin taped to the board. The essay comes back with a good mark, and the first-year tells everyone.|You help for nothing. They try to pay you anyway, and you won't let them.|You leave the coin where it is, as always. Somebody has drawn a small sun beside the notice.}

@event job_board_greenhouse_pots
title: Pots for Old Maud
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: leaf
tags: job money
scene: A greenhouse doorway stacked with muddy clay pots, an old gardener on a stool directing a student with a scrubbing brush.
text: {Old Maud's notice asks for clay pots scrubbed before planting, for a small wage and a cup of tea.|Maud's notice again. More pots, the same tea.|Maud has given up pinning notices. The pots are just waiting at the greenhouse door, with your brush on top.}
> left: Scrub them for the wage
  gold +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {A hundred pots, scrubbed and stacked. Maud pays you and says you're slower than her and twice as thorough.|You scrub them all and drink the tea. The wage is small and honestly earned.|You scrub in silence, and so does she. She pays you and lets you take a cutting home.}
> right: Scrub them and ask about the plants
  gold +tiny
  alchemy +small
  say: {Maud talks while you scrub, about which leaf cures what and which leaf kills. She pays a little less, because of the talking.|You learn three remedies and a poison you should never brew. The wage is smaller; the lesson isn't.|Maud tests you on last month's lesson while you scrub. You pass, mostly. The wage is small and the tea is good.}
